  <section class="burn-section">
    <h2>Which rules apply to you</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Elder Mountain sits on the line between Marion and Hamilton counties, and each side has different rules. The fire station on Cumberland Road is on the Marion County side. Much of the Hamilton County side is inside Chattanooga city limits. If you are not sure which side you are on, check your property tax bill or <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">ask us</a>.</p>
    <div class="juris-grid">
      <div class="juris">
        <h3>Marion County side</h3>
        <p class="juris-season" id="tdf-season"></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Permit:</strong> a free burn permit from the Tennessee Division of Forestry from October 15 to May 15, for any leaf, brush, or debris pile within 500 feet of woods or grass. That covers nearly everywhere on the mountain.</li>
          <li><strong>How to get one:</strong> <a href="https://tn.firesponse.com/burn-permit/public-restrictions/">online</a>, in the MyTN app, or by phone at <a href="tel:+18773502876">877-350-BURN (2876)</a>, Monday to Friday, 8:00 AM to 4:30 PM Central.</li>
          <li><strong>Good for:</strong> the day it is issued. The fire must be completely out by midnight.</li>
          <li><strong>You may burn:</strong> leaves, brush, limbs, and untreated wood grown on your property. Never household trash, paper, cardboard, plastic, tires, shingles, or building debris.</li>
          <li><strong>No permit needed:</strong> a campfire, a grill, or a burn barrel with a cover of half-inch mesh screen, unless a burn ban is in effect.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small">Sources: <a href="https://www.tn.gov/tnwildlandfire/prevention/safe-debris-burning/permits.html">Tennessee Division of Forestry</a>, <a href="https://www.tn.gov/environment/air/open-burning.html">TDEC open burning rules</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="juris">
        <h3>Hamilton County side</h3>
        <p class="juris-season" id="apcb-season"></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Permit:</strong> a seasonal burn permit from the Chattanooga-Hamilton County Air Pollution Control Bureau. It is $75 inside Chattanooga city limits (with an inspection, allow up to 7 business days) and $15 in unincorporated Hamilton County.</li>
          <li><strong>Season:</strong> October 1 to April 30. All open burning, including burn barrels, is banned in Hamilton County from May 1 to September 30.</li>
          <li><strong>Every burn day:</strong> check that the bureau says burning is allowed today (shown above, or text <em>burn</em> to <a href="sms:+14236435971">423-643-5971</a>), burn only during the posted hours, and call your local fire department before you light.</li>
          <li><strong>You may burn:</strong> vegetation and clean, untreated wood no more than 12 inches across, from the permit address only. Keep the pile to 8 by 8 by 8 feet. No stumps, paper, cardboard, treated wood, or trash.</li>
          <li><strong>No permit needed:</strong> a recreational fire of clean, seasoned firewood no bigger than 3 feet wide and 2 feet high.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small">Source: <a href="https://apcb.org/burning-permits/">Air Pollution Control Bureau</a>, 423-643-5970.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
