  <section class="burn-section">
    <h2>Before you light</h2>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Get the permit for your side of the mountain, and check this page and the official status the same day.</li>
      <li>Do not burn when it is windy, when the humidity is low, or when gusts are forecast. Put the fire out if the wind picks up.</li>
      <li>Clear a firebreak to bare dirt around the pile: at least 3 feet through leaves or short grass, 5 feet through tall grass or brush.</li>
      <li>Keep piles small and add to them a little at a time. Stay clear of overhanging limbs and power lines.</li>
      <li>Have a charged hose or water and a rake or shovel at the pile.</li>
      <li>Stay with the fire until it is completely out. Mornings and early evenings are usually calmest.</li>
      <li>If a fire gets away from you, <strong>call 911 right away.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">Burning without a permit, leaving a fire unattended near woods, or letting a fire escape onto someone else's property are crimes in Tennessee, and you can be billed for the cost of putting the fire out. Sources: <a href="https://www.tn.gov/tnwildlandfire/prevention/safe-debris-burning/tn-wildfire-fire-laws.html">Tennessee wildfire laws</a>, <a href="https://www.tn.gov/tnwildlandfire/prevention/safe-debris-burning/burn-bans.html">burn bans and restrictions</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="burn-section how">
    <h2>How the index works</h2>
    <p>The Elder Mountain burn index adds three scores, each from 1 to 6, so the total runs from 3 to 18. It is based on the BAFDRS fire danger index from FST Dynamics, which runs the Elder East weather station.</p>
    <table class="points">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Points</th><th scope="col">Wind gust</th><th scope="col">Humidity</th><th scope="col">10-hour fuel moisture</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>5 mph or less</td><td>50% or more</td><td>20% or more</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>6–10 mph</td><td>40–49%</td><td>17–19%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>11–15 mph</td><td>30–39%</td><td>14–16%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>16–20 mph</td><td>20–29%</td><td>11–13%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>21–25 mph</td><td>10–19%</td><td>8–10%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td>over 25 mph</td><td>under 10%</td><td>under 8%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>3–5 is very low, 6–8 low, 9–11 moderate, 12–15 high, and 16–18 very high. We say <strong>do not burn</strong> at high or above, or whenever there is a state burn ban, a Red Flag Warning, a Fire Weather Watch, a Wind Advisory, gusts of 20 mph or more, or humidity under 25%. Ten-hour fuel moisture is the moisture in sticks about the thickness of a finger, the fuel that carries a fire across the ground.</p>
    <p class="small">This page is guidance from the Elder Mountain Fire Department. It is not a permit and does not replace the official status from the Tennessee Division of Forestry or the Air Pollution Control Bureau. Data: <a href="https://mesonet.fstdynamics.com/">FST Dynamics mesonet</a>, <a href="https://fems.fs2c.usda.gov/">USDA Forest Service FEMS</a>, <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mrx/">National Weather Service Morristown</a>, <a href="https://apcb.org/">Air Pollution Control Bureau</a>, Tennessee Department of Agriculture.</p>
  </section>
